@@ -1,5 +1,13 @@                                                                                               
 # Living Survey: Artificial_General_Intelligence                                                              
                                                                                                               
 ## Introduction                                                                                               
                                                                                                               
 This document collects the scientific literature regarding **Artificial_General_Intelligence**.               
+                                                                                                              
+## Safety, Ethics, and Policy                                                                                 
+                                                                                                              
+Recent discourse emphasizes the need for conceptual discipline and rigorous safety protocols in AGI           
development. The European Commission's call for AGI in healthcare has sparked debate regarding the risks of    
undefined systems and the precautionary principle, highlighting the necessity for clear regulatory             
frameworks[^10.1016_j.jrt.2026.100183]. Furthermore, critical analyses point out that the pursuit of AGI is    
sometimes intertwined with normative frameworks rooted in historical eugenics, urging researchers to focus on  
well-defined tasks with established safety protocols rather than pursuing an all-knowing                       
system[^10.5210_fm.v29i4.13636]. Formal verification and mechanistic interpretability are proposed as essential
pathways to guarantee provably safe and controllable AGI systems[^10.48550_arxiv.2309.01933].                  
+                                                                                                              
+## Theoretical Foundations and Consciousness                                                                  
+                                                                                                              
+Theoretical explorations into AGI increasingly intersect with cognitive science and consciousness studies.    
Research into the topology of mind proposes a morphogenetic continuity framework, defining mental states as    
rigorous structures emerging from compatible configurations and sensitive constraints, ultimately arguing for  
the mathematical inevitability of autonomous general intelligence[^10.5281_zenodo.19034234]. A duplicate       
submission reinforces this topological approach to autonomous intelligence[^10.5281_zenodo.19034233]. In       
parallel, investigations into robot consciousness examine whether sufficiently advanced general robotic        
intelligence could be indistinguishable from human consciousness, exploring implications for theory of mind,   
free will, and alternative cosmological ontologies[^10.22339_jbh.v7i4.7403].                                   
+                                                                                                              
+## Global Research and Strategic Development                                                                  
+                                                                                                              
+Strategic national initiatives are actively mapping pathways toward general AI. China's national strategy to  
lead in AI by 2030 includes dedicated tracking of institutions pursuing general AI, machine learning, and      
brain-inspired architectures[^10.51593_20210064]. Broader philosophical and psychological perspectives examine 
the future of intelligence research in the age of AI, discussing transhumanism, cognitive enhancements, and the
promises and perils of super-intelligence, while questioning how psychology can contribute to such             
developments[^10.1016_j.intell.2021.101563].                                                                   
+                                                                                                              
+## Architectural Frameworks                                                                                   
+                                                                                                              
+Practical frameworks for AGI are being explored through advanced control systems. Deep reinforcement learning 
is proposed as a general-purpose, application-independent framework for AGI, specifically applied to high-level
intelligent robot control architectures that separate intelligence generation from low-level kinematic         
execution[^10.48550_arxiv.2105.13806]. A related study reiterates this vision-based learning framework for     
intelligent robot control using deep reinforcement learning as a foundational AGI                              
application[^10.48550_arxiv.2105.13806].                                                                       
                                                                                                               

